Drives its own private GC state (its own `gcBindMemory`/`gcInit`-bound arena and
its own bare `reserveInit` descriptor), and every assertion is about that
private reserve: span alignment, the chunk list, and what `gcDeinit` gives back.
Objects it allocates are the private collector's, invisible to the ambient one,
and it deliberately never collects them so that the reserve is forced to grow.

CORRECTION: "None of them depends on whether the ambient (libbitrt) collector
ever runs" was true in spirit but the file went further and implied the
ambient collector effectively does not fire — it does. Measured, aarch64-macos,
5 runs each policy: default `collections=0` every time; `BIT_GC=stress`
22318, 22328, 22326, 22333, 22325 (denominator: 5/5, spread ~0.07%) — well
above stress.bit's documented ~101 scheduler-loop floor, so most of this
count is this program's own allocation-loop back-edges. Confirmed default and
`BIT_GC=stress` stdout are both byte-identical to `.expected`.

The backing `desc`/`arena`/`indexArena` (ordinary managed `[]i64` slices) are
each used once, at their `reserveInit`/`gcBindMemory` call, and never
referenced again in the source by name — the same shape #4097 found let
gcbit.bit's own backing slices get swept out from under it (a real segfault
under BIT_GC=stress, fixed there with an explicit trailing read). `desc` is
`checkSpans`'s own local over the standalone reserve API, not bound through
`gcBindMemory`, and is out of #4184's scope; `main`'s own arena/indexArena/
heapBlock/heapLock/lock/state/meta/bigMeta are what #4184 covers.

#4184 RESOLVED: measured this file's own compiled stack maps directly (the
compiler's emitted __bit_gc entries for `main`) and found its current
codegen keeps every one of those bindings precisely rooted through the
whole ~22k-collection OBJS loop anyway — an accident of conservative
interval widening, not a documented guarantee. The `rooted` field in the
final print, mirroring gcbit's `alive` and gcspanblocks'/gcspanfree's/
gcspanfreelist's own `rooted`, closes the gap for real regardless of
whether that accident continues to hold across future compiler changes.
